    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this research, we develop an algorithm to extract the geometry of exhumed folds using Light Detection And Ranging (LiDAR) high-resolution topographic data. In cases where folds have been unroofed by erosion, this technology presents a means of quantifying a structure’s geometry at lower cost and higher resolution than may be possible using methods such as active-source seismic surveys. LiDAR technology is capable of producing Digital Elevation Models (DEMs) with spatial resolution of up to one meter over broad (10s – 100s of km2) areas. Thus, in cases where the fold’s geometry is recorded in the topography, LiDAR may provide a means of producing a high-resolution model of a structure’s geometry. In the past, LiDAR DEMs have primarily been used to study landscape processes; however, these topographic data may be applied to structural geology studies that seek to quantify fold shape at high resolution in three dimensions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
